From b049b075a3e00c2f8e7c3bfc8ed91234fff7b57c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Wolfram Huesken <woh@m18.io>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 15:55:28 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 01/13] fix(agent): apply agent.olium.max_turns to
 non-autopilot engine runs
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

buildOliumEngineWithSpec built the olium engine with MaxTurns from the
per-call SessionSpec only, and every shared-engine caller — triage
(RunTriageLoop), swarm phases, source analysis, query — passes no per-call
cap. spec.MaxTurns was therefore always 0 and oengine.New fell back to its
hardcoded 32, so agent.olium.max_turns was parsed, defaulted and documented
but read by nothing: an operator raising it to 128 still got 32 turns.

A 25-finding autopilot triage batch needs far more than 32 turns (it
re-verifies source, stored records and live probes per finding), and the
ceiling surfaces as a plain error where autopilot's own loop treats it as a
budget stop — so the batch failed and took the whole triage round with it:

    [triage] aborted: triage round 0 batch 1 failed:
    agent execution failed: olium: exceeded max turns (32)

Fall back to cfg.MaxTurns when SessionSpec.MaxTurns is unset. Per-call caps
(guardrail 1, intent 30, verifier) still win, and 0 at both levels leaves the
engine default of 32 in place. TestMaxTurnsResolution drives a real engine
against an OpenAI-compatible httptest endpoint that always answers with a
tool call, pinning all three levels by the ceiling that actually fires.
